Watched 3Blue1Brown neural network chapters 1 and 2.
Loss: one number for how wrong the model is. Lower is better.
Gradient descent: change each weight a small step in the direction that lowers the loss. Rule: new x = old x minus lr times slope.
Slope: shows which way the loss grows, so I move the opposite way.
Learning rate (lr): the step size I choose. Too big overshoots, too small is slow.
Example: loss y = (x minus 3) squared. With lr 0.1, x goes 0, 0.6, 1.08, 1.464 toward 3. With lr 1.0 it bounces between 0 and 6. With lr 1.1 it blows up.
Mistake: I found the line x = x minus lr times slope confusing. In Python "=" means calculate the right side, then store it. I fixed this by doing the arithmetic by hand.

10/10
Neuron: sum of (input times weight) plus bias, then an activation.
Weight: how much one input matters to the neuron. Bias: a constant added to the sum.
Activation: a function applied to the sum. ReLU keeps the sum if it is positive, otherwise 0. Without activations, stacked layers collapse into one straight line.
Overfitting: the model memorizes training images. Sign: train accuracy rises while validation stalls or drops. Underfitting: both stay low.
Training set is for learning, validation set is a mock test for comparing ideas, test set is the hidden final exam.
Created my GitHub repo Int_SIG-REC.
Mistakes: I said a weight is how important the neuron is (correct: how important one input is). I called activation a number put into the neuron (correct: a function applied to the sum). I said a ReLU neuron with sum 3 outputs 1 (correct: 3). A later check with sum minus 5 gave 0, which is right.

11/10
Softmax turns raw scores into probabilities that add up to 1.
Cross entropy loss: minus ln of the probability given to the true class. Confident and right gives a small loss, confident and wrong gives a big one. Equal scores give equal probabilities, meaning the model is unsure.
Read the starter kit: 900 train and 240 validation images, 6 classes. The model outputs raw scores because CrossEntropyLoss already includes softmax. Images are normalized with the ImageNet mean and std, and the verifier does the same.
Started convolution: a kernel is a small grid of weights. At each position, multiply matching cells and add, which gives one number. Sliding over the image gives a feature map. The same weights are reused everywhere (weight sharing).

12/10
A kernel multiplies cell by cell and adds. It is not matrix multiplication. Output size = (input + 2 times padding minus kernel) divided by stride, plus 1.
A layer has many kernels, so it makes many feature maps. Kernel numbers are learned by gradient descent. Nobody tells the network to find edges. It only lowers the final loss.
Max pooling keeps the largest value in each block. Global average pooling averages each feature map into one number. The final layer turns those numbers into one score per class.
Batch: a group of 32 images used for one weight update. Epoch: one pass over all 900 training images, about 29 batches.
A kernel reading many feature maps has one flat sheet per input map and makes one output map.
Weights are like dials turned to lower the loss. Backpropagation is the chain rule applied from the loss backwards.
